<h2>Before you accept the insurer’s estimate</h2>
<p>Rural and small-town claims often run into the same problems: a quick inspection that misses outbuildings, a roof estimate that only covers repairs when the damage is widespread, or depreciation that is never released because the policyholder did not know to request it. Before you sign off on a payment, it helps to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Compare the estimate line by line with your own photos and contractor findings.</li>
<li>Confirm every damaged structure is listed, including fences and barns.</li>
<li>Ask whether your policy pays replacement cost or actual cash value, and how recoverable depreciation is released.</li>
<li>Check your wind/hail deductible on the declarations page.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> services cover these issues.</p>

<h2>Permits in Lockhart</h2>
<p>The City of Lockhart has its own permit office, and its rules are separate from the City of San Marcos Permit Center. Check with the City of Lockhart before roof replacement or structural repair. Properties outside city limits in unincorporated Caldwell County follow county requirements. Whether your insurer pays for code-required upgrades depends on your policy’s ordinance or law coverage; our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes guide</a> explains the general rules.</p>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster helps in Lockhart</h2>
